# Judge item J1752

Does the finding describe the labelled defect? `describes`, `partially`, or `does-not-describe`.

## Finding

``` text
head/.claude/adapters/loa_cheval/config/endpoint_policy.py:151 — `validate_providers` admits `allow_insecure_endpoint` for a provider of any `type`, but the request-time half of the rule (`TransportPolicy`: no proxy env, no redirects, path prefix) is only ever constructed in `providers/openai_adapter.py` (`_transport_policy`, `health_check`); the anthropic/google/bedrock adapters are untouched. A `type: anthropic` (or google) provider with `endpoint: http://10.x…` plus an opt-in therefore loads cleanly and dispatches over plain http honouring `HTTP_PROXY` and following redirects — exactly what this module's docstring (lines 26-28) and Example 4 in `.loa.config.yaml.example` promise cannot happen. Restrict the opt-in to `openai`/`openai_compat` at load (or refuse other types that carry it).
head/.claude/skills/bridgebuilder-review/resources/config.ts:726 — `isLocalVoice` (and `applyLocality`/`singleModelLocalityRefusal` built on it, used by `main.ts:486,506`) classifies a voice as local from `dispatch_group` alone and never looks at the model entry's `fallback_chain`. `hop-locality.py` (lines 123-130, the rule Flatline and the dissent use in this same PR, asserted by LOC-7) deliberately classifies a local entry whose fallback chain reaches a hosted/unresolved hop as hosted because cheval walks that chain inside one call. Under `hounfour.locality: local_voices` Bridgebuilder keeps such a voice and cheval may dispatch to a hosted provider — the leak D-8.1 says is refused by name …
```

